= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Apply the <exponential-symbol order criterion for a multistep method>. The exact-solution residual of the <linear multistep method> is encoded by $E(z)=\rho(e^z)-z\sigma(e^z)$; vanishing through degree $p$ is equivalent to the <order conditions for a linear multistep method> through order $p$. Here $\rho(1)=0$, $\rho'(1)=\sigma(1)=1-\alpha$, and direct <Taylor expansion> gives
$$
E(z)=-\frac{1+\alpha}{24}z^4-\frac{17+13\alpha}{360}z^5+O(z^6).
$$
For example, for $n\geq2$ the coefficient of $z^n$ can be calculated as
$$
\frac{2^n-(1+\alpha)}{n!}
-\frac{\tfrac23(1-\alpha)+2^{n-1}(5+\alpha)/12}{(n-1)!}.
$$
For $n=2,3$ this is zero, verifying the lower conditions explicitly. The constant and linear coefficients also vanish for every $\alpha$. Therefore **the formal order is at least three for every $\alpha$**.

The fourth-degree term vanishes exactly when $\alpha=-1$. For that value the fifth-degree coefficient is $-1/90\ne0$, so
$$
\boxed{p=3\text{ if }\alpha\ne-1,\qquad p=4\text{ precisely when }\alpha=-1.}
$$
At $\alpha=1$ these are formal residual conditions only: the <characteristic polynomial> has a double root at one, so the method is not convergent. Canceling its common polynomial factor would change the recurrence and give a different order calculation. This distinction is relevant to part (b).

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Consistency was established in part (a). By the <Dahlquist equivalence theorem>, convergence additionally requires the <root condition for a multistep method>. Since $\rho(w)=(w-1)(w-\alpha)$, its roots lie in the closed <unit disk> with each unit-modulus root simple exactly for
$$
\boxed{-1\leq\alpha<1.}
$$
The endpoint $\alpha=-1$ is <zero-stability> although it has a simple parasitic root at minus one; $\alpha=1$ has a repeated root at one and is excluded.

For every parameter in this convergent range, the leading coefficient of $\sigma$ is $(5+\alpha)/12>0$, while
$$
\sigma(-1)=-(1-\alpha)/3<0.
$$
Since $\sigma(w)\to+\infty$ as $w\to-\infty$, it has a real root $w_*<-1$. Its other real root lies between minus one and one because $\sigma(1)=1-\alpha>0$, so the exterior root is simple.

On the <Dahlquist test equation>, the <amplification polynomial of a multistep method> is $P_z(w)=\rho(w)-z\sigma(w)$, with $z=h\lambda$. For $|z|\to\infty$, the equivalent polynomial $-P_z/z=\sigma-\rho/z$ tends coefficientwise to $\sigma$, and its leading coefficient stays nonzero. Choose a small closed disk about $w_*$ lying wholly outside the <unit disk> and containing no other root of $\sigma$. On its boundary $|\sigma|$ has a positive minimum, while $|\rho/z|$ is smaller for every sufficiently large $|z|$. <Rouché's theorem> then puts one root of $P_z$ inside this exterior disk for all such complex $z$.

That root violates the stability <root condition for a multistep method>. Hence there is a finite radius $R_\alpha$ beyond which no point belongs to the <absolute stability> domain:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal S_\alpha\subseteq\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|\leq R_\alpha\},\qquad-1\leq\alpha<1.}
$$
This proves boundedness in every complex direction, rather than only excluding <A-stability>. It is an instance of <exterior roots of the derivative polynomial bound multistep stability>.

